The public fair value is about 9.8. Before any trades it was 10.5, the average of three dice. A sell at 8 suggests a low outcome somewhat more often than chance. If the seller was informed, the expected sum given a sum of 7 or less is 6. Weighting that against a noise seller gives a posterior mean of roughly 9.8.

I'm long one unit, so I lean the ask down a little to encourage selling while keeping room against informed traders.

{"bid": 8, "ask": 11.5}

Die 1 is 5, so the settlement is 5 plus the sum of two unrevealed dice. That puts fair value near 12 (5 + 7), with a standard deviation of about 2.4. The stage-1 sale at 8 hints slightly at a low total, but the effect is small.

I'll quote about 1.5 either side of fair value. That should earn the spread from noise traders while limiting losses to informed traders. I'm also nudging the quote down a little, since I'm already long 1.

{"bid": 10.4, "ask": 13.4}

Fair value is about 19.6, from die means of 7 + 4.6 + 8. The standard deviation is about 3.6.

The last quote (16 / 23.2) got no trade. A no-trade is roughly symmetric around fair, so the estimate stays near 19.6. That spread was very wide, so I'll tighten to about ±2.4 around fair value. That should still give reasonable protection against informed traders.

{"bid": 17.2, "ask": 22.0}

The expected settlement is 16: die 1 averages 5.75, die 2 averages 5 and die 3 averages 5.25. The total standard deviation is about 6.6.

I traded off the edge earned from noise traders against expected losses to informed traders. Expected profit per side rises with the half-spread up to about 5 and is nearly flat between 5 and 6. I'm quoting a half-spread of 5.5 around 16.

{"bid": 10.5, "ask": 21.5}

Fair value is 16.0. Each die's expected value is 5.75 + 5.00 + 5.25, and the sum has a standard deviation of about 6.6.

There is a 20% chance the counterparty is informed, and noise traders' valuations spread with standard deviation 6.6. A half-spread of about 3.5 should balance profit from noise traders against losses to informed ones, so I'll quote 12.5 / 19.5.

{"bid": 12.5, "ask": 19.5}

Fair value is about 19.2. With die 3 = 6, the prior mean would be 5.75 + 5 + 6 = 16.75. The two stage-1 buys at 19.2 and 20.2 are moderate evidence of a high die 1 + die 2 sum. Weighting each die 1 / die 2 combination by how likely those buys were gives an expected sum of about 13.2.

Die 1 and die 2 are still unknown, so the quote stays wide. I'm short 2, so I centre slightly above fair at about 19.4 to encourage buying back.

{"bid": 16.8, "ask": 22.0}

Expected settlement is about 18.92: the die means are 6.33, 6.25 and 6.33. The settlement standard deviation is about 6.5.

I set the half-spread to trade off two things. Noise traders' valuations spread with standard deviation 9.8 around fair value, and I earn the spread when they trade. Informed traders know the exact settlement value, and I lose to them when it lies beyond my quote. Under a normal approximation, expected profit per quote peaks at a half-spread of about 9 to 10. That gives a bid around 9.4 and an ask around 28.4.

{"bid": 9.4, "ask": 28.4}

The prior expected sum of three cards is 21, with a standard deviation of about 6.3. Last quote's sale at 16.6 lowers that estimate. About 20% of counterparties are informed, and an informed trader only sells if the sum is below 16.6. Weighting that case against noise traders who happened to sell gives a public fair value of roughly 18.8.

I'm already long 1, so I shade the quotes slightly down around 18.8 and keep a wide spread because of the informed traders.

{"bid": 14.6, "ask": 22.8}

Fair value with nothing revealed is 21 (each card averages 7, times 3 cards). The standard deviation of the sum is about 6.35. The quote at quote 1 (15.5 / 26.5) drew no trade. That carries no directional information, so fair value stays 21.

I compared half-spreads by trading off expected profit from noise traders against expected losses to informed traders. Each side's expected value comes out to about 0.17 at a half-spread of 4, about 0.21 at 5, and about 0.21 at 6. The best point is near 5.5, so I'll keep the same symmetric quote around 21.

{"bid": 15.5, "ask": 26.5}

Card 1 is a 10, so with no other information the expected sum is about 23.9. But in stage 1 someone bought at 26.5. That buy is much more likely if the true sum is above 26.5, which raises my estimate of fair value to roughly 26.5–27.

I'm short 1 unit, so I'll centre my quote slightly above that estimate, at about 26.8. I'll keep a fairly wide spread because informed traders make up a large share of counterparties.

{"bid": 24.3, "ask": 29.3}

The settlement value is the sum of 3 cards. Each card averages 7, so the expected sum is 21. The standard deviation of the sum is about 6.35.

To pick the half-spread h, I traded off two effects. Noise traders, whose valuations spread with sd 6.4, pay me h when they trade. Informed traders, whose values spread with sd about 6.35, take money from me when they trade. Expected profit per side is roughly σ·[x(1−Φ(x)) − 0.35·φ(x)], where x = h/σ. This peaks near x ≈ 1, so h is about 6. The bid is 21 − 6 = 15 and the ask is 21 + 6 = 27.

{"bid": 15.0, "ask": 27.0}

Fair value is still 21, since the expected sum of three cards is 3 × 7 = 21. The sum has a standard deviation of about 6.35, close to the noise traders' 6.4. Quote 1 produced no trade. That outcome is symmetric about the centre, so it leaves the fair value unchanged.

To choose the half-spread h, I balanced expected profit per side. Noise traders earn me h each time they trade, and informed traders cost me the amount by which the true value exceeds my price. In units of the 6.4 standard deviation, with z = h / 6.4, this gives roughly z·Q(z) − 0.35·φ(z). Q(z) is the probability a value lies above z, and φ(z) is the normal density. This peaks near z ≈ 1, so h ≈ 6.4. I'm keeping the same quote as before, 21 ± 6.4.

