Card 1 is a 2, so the prior fair value is about 16.2. That is 2 plus two cards drawn from the remaining 51, which average about 7.1 each. Someone then bought at my 21.7 ask. An informed buyer would only do that if the true sum were above 21.7, so I raise my estimate to roughly 17.8.

I quote about ±3.5 around 17.8. I skew slightly upward because I am short one unit.

{"bid": 14.8, "ask": 21.8}

Card 3's unconditional mean is 8.93, which would put fair value near 19.9. The earlier trades pull that down. Both sells at my bid (20.1 and 18.4) are much more likely if card 3 is low. Weighting each possible card by those two trades gives an expected card 3 of about 6.0, so fair value is about 17.

I'm long 2, so I want to lean toward selling. The ask sits just above 18, so informed traders only buy when the sum is 19 or more. The bid is low enough to avoid buying much more.

{"bid": 15.0, "ask": 18.1}

Card 1 = 10 leaves 16 cards summing to 135, so the naive fair value is 10 + 2×8.44 ≈ 26.9. Someone sold to me at 21.8, which suggests the remaining two cards may be low. That lowers the public fair value to roughly 25–26.

I'm long 1, so I'll keep the spread wide against informed traders and shade both prices slightly down to help unload the long.

{"bid": 21.8, "ask": 29.0}

Fair value is about 23.8. Each card averages 119/15 ≈ 7.93, and three cards give 3 × 7.93 ≈ 23.8. The standard deviation of the sum is about 9.4. Last quote's no-trade tells us little, so I keep fair value at 23.8.

Half the counterparties are informed, so quoting tight loses money to them. A rough normal approximation suggests the edge only becomes positive at about ±10 to 12 around fair value. I'll keep the quote wide, roughly symmetric around 23.8.

{"bid": 13.5, "ask": 34.2}

The deck's average card is 8.25, so before any trades the two-card sum was worth about 16.5. Someone sold to us at 11.5. That is strong evidence the sum is low, because only 14 of the 120 possible pairs sum to 11 or less. Updating on that sale moves the fair value to about 12.8. The likely values now split into a low group around 9.6 and a high group around 17.4.

Given that two-way uncertainty and the informed traders, I'm quoting wide around 12.8. Since we are already long 1, I'm shading the quote slightly lower.

{"bid": 9.4, "ask": 16.2}

The expected sum of 3 cards is 3 × 7.8 = 23.4, with a standard deviation of about 7.2.

Half the counterparties know the exact settlement value, so a tight spread loses more to them than it earns from noise traders. With a normal approximation, a half-spread of about 8 is roughly where the two effects balance and the expected value turns positive. That gives a bid of 15.4 and an ask of 31.4.

{"bid": 15.4, "ask": 31.4}

Card 1 is a 2, which is the only 2 in the deck, so the minimum is certainly 2 and the contract settles at exactly 2. An informed trader values it at 2 and will not trade if my bid is below 2 and my ask is above 2.

The only counterparty who might trade is a noise trader. Their valuation is Normal with mean 2 and standard deviation 6.3. On each side, expected profit is x × P(noise > x), where x is the distance from 2. This is largest at about x ≈ 0.75σ ≈ 4.7. That gives a bid of 2 − 4.7 = −2.7 and an ask of 2 + 4.7 = 6.7.

{"bid": -2.7, "ask": 6.7}

Prior fair value (expected max of 3 cards) is about 11.98. The buy at 13.2 is mild evidence that the max is 15, so the posterior fair value is about 12.7.

Since I'm short 1, I'll skew the quotes slightly up around 12.7. I'll keep the spread wide because the outcome is close to binary: either the max is 15, or it is 11 or less.

{"bid": 11.5, "ask": 13.9}

The coin must be the fair one, because a P(heads)=1 coin cannot produce the tail on flip 2. So settlement is 1 + Binomial(4, 0.5), with values 1–5, mean 3 and sd 1. The no-trade at 1.6/4.4 makes values 2–4 a bit more likely than 1 or 5, but the mean stays at 3 by symmetry.

The informed trader only hurts me when the value is 1 or 5, each about 3.5% likely after that update. Quoting outside 2 and 4 captures most of the noise-trader edge, and expected profit on each side peaks around 1.3 from fair. That gives roughly 1.7 / 4.3.

{"bid": 1.7, "ask": 4.3}

Prior expected heads is 8 × 0.61 = 4.88, so the public fair value is about 48.8. The settlement value is bimodal, though. If the 0.9 coin was picked, the value is around 72. If the 0.3 coin was picked, it is around 24.

Wide quotes beat a tight spread here. An ask near 76 is only hit by informed traders when the value is 80. A bid at 20 is only hit by informed traders when the value is 10 or less. Noise traders (sd 26) still reach both levels often enough. My rough estimate is about +2 expected profit per quote on each side.

{"bid": 20, "ask": 76}

Fair value is about 24.8. Each flip comes up heads with probability 0.6·0.5 + 0.4·0.8 = 0.62. Over 8 flips that is 4.96 expected heads, and 5 × 4.96 = 24.8.

The settlement value is quite uncertain, with a standard deviation of about 8.8. That gives informed traders a large edge over a tight quote. A half-spread of about 6 balances what I earn from noise traders against what I lose to informed traders.

{"bid": 18.8, "ask": 30.8}

Expected P(heads) is 0.3×0.2 + 0.4×0.5 + 0.3×0.7 = 0.47, so the fair value is 7 × 0.47 ≈ 3.29.

The settlement value has a standard deviation of about 1.83. Noise traders have more spread than that (standard deviation 2.7), but 20% of counterparties know the exact value. To protect against them, I quote roughly ±1.1 around fair value.

{"bid": 2.2, "ask": 4.4}

The prior expected heads is 7 × 0.47 = 3.29. Someone bought at 4.4, and that is weak evidence the count is high (5 or more heads). Updating on it moves the public fair value to about 3.7.

I'm short 1, so I'll centre my quote near 3.7 and skew it slightly upward, with enough spread to cover adverse selection from informed traders.

