Expected P(heads) is 0.4 × 0.5 + 0.6 × 0.7 = 0.62. So the fair value is 7 × 0.62 × 10 = **43.4**.

The settlement value is uncertain, with a standard deviation of about 14. That spread comes from:
- the flip-to-flip randomness, which contributes a variance of about 1.58 heads²;
- not knowing which coin it is, which adds about 0.47 heads².

Informed traders make up 35% of counterparties and will pick off a tight quote. Noise traders' valuations have a standard deviation of 11 around 43.4. I'll quote about 7 points either side of fair value to balance profit from noise traders against losses to informed ones.

{"bid": 36.5, "ask": 50.3}

Before any reveals, the expected chance of heads is 0.4×0.5 + 0.6×0.7 = 0.62. Over 7 flips that is 4.34 expected heads, so fair value is about 43.4.

The last quote (37/50) drew no trade, which tells us almost nothing, so fair value stays near 43.4. The settlement value has a wide spread (standard deviation roughly 13). Informed traders (35% of arrivals) know the exact value and will pick me off on a narrow quote. Quoting about 6 either side of fair value balances the spread earned from noise traders against losses to informed ones.

{"bid": 37.4, "ask": 49.4}

Fair value is 17.5. The expected heads probability is 0.2·0.2 + 0.3·0.7 + 0.5·0.9 = 0.70, so the expected value is 5 flips × 0.70 × 5 = 17.5.

The settlement value is uncertain: it has a standard deviation of about 7.8. One trader in five is informed, so I quote about 4 either side of fair value. That keeps losses to informed traders small while still trading often with noise traders, whose valuations have standard deviation 12.

