Given draw 1 = red, the posterior on the number of red balls is proportional to r. That gives an expected value of 7.

The stage-1 flow points higher. There were two buys at about 7 and one sell at 4.3, which is consistent with informed buyers. A rough Bayesian update on that flow moves the estimate to about 8. I'm short 1, so I lean the quote slightly upward.

{"bid": 7.0, "ask": 9.1}

With no red in the first three draws, the urn alone puts fair value near 16. The trading history moves it up. The buy at 56 in stage 2 is fairly strong evidence of an informed trader, which happens only if there are at least 6 red balls. The earlier no-trades pull the other way, mainly against 0, 1 or 6+ red. Combining them gives a posterior mean of about 2.8 red balls, so fair value is roughly 26–28.

Possible settlement values are spaced 10 apart, so the quote edges should fall between them. A bid of 15 loses to an informed seller only when there are 0 or 1 red balls. An ask of 44 loses to an informed buyer only when there are 5 or more red balls, which has about 19% posterior probability. I am short 1, so I lean slightly toward buying back.

{"bid": 15, "ask": 44}

Two red draws out of two make the posterior on R proportional to R(R−1), so the expected value is E[R] = 8 and the public fair value is **80**. The earlier buy at 114 was above the maximum possible settlement of 100, so it must have come from a noise trader and tells us nothing new.

Weighing the profit from noise traders against losses to informed traders, the best ask is about 115 and the best bid is about 47–48.

